<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dresden Codex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Codex Dresdensis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> Codex is a Maya book of the 11th or 12th century, probably from the region of Chichén Itzá in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and one of four Maya hieroglyphic books to survive. Painted by eight scribes on folded bark paper, it contains Venus tables, lunar tables linked to eclipses and ritual calendars. It was long thought the oldest surviving book from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, until the Maya Codex of Mexico proved older in 2018. Bought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1739, it is held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> State Library in Dresden and was badly damaged by water in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dresden-codex.jpg" alt="Dresden Codex" width="320" height="693" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maya scribes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dresden_codex,_page_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
